const faq = [
  { q: 'Are there ravens in Yellowstone National Park?', a: 'Yes — Common Ravens are year-round residents throughout Yellowstone. They are one of the most visible large birds in the park, particularly in winter when they congregate at wolf and predator kills. The park\'s extensive wildlife population (elk, bison, wolves, bears) provides abundant carrion, supporting a thriving resident raven population and attracting wintering non-breeding flocks.' },
  { q: 'Do Yellowstone ravens follow wolves?', a: 'Yes. The relationship between Yellowstone ravens and wolves is one of the most studied raven–predator interactions in North America. Research by Stahler, Heinrich, and Crabtree (2002) documented that ravens follow and harass hunting wolves, attend kills within minutes of the first wolf feeding, and obtain significant quantities of food from wolf kills — particularly from elk. Ravens also appear to play — engaging in aerial acrobatics and object-dropping near wolves and sometimes landing near resting packs without apparent fear.' },
  { q: 'How many ravens are in Yellowstone?', a: 'No precise census of Yellowstone ravens exists, but population estimates based on survey data suggest hundreds of resident birds year-round, with significant influxes of wintering non-breeding birds. The reintroduction of wolves to Yellowstone in 1995 is widely believed to have benefited the raven population by increasing the availability of large carrion — wolf kills provide accessible food for ravens that would not be available from natural ungulate mortality alone.' },
];
