    <h1>Ravens in Yellowstone: Wolf-Following and Winter Survival</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Yellowstone National Park hosts one of North America's best-studied raven populations. Ravens here are closely associated with the park's wolf packs — following hunts, arriving at kills within minutes, and obtaining substantial food from wolf-killed elk and bison. The wolf reintroduction in 1995 is believed to have increased carrion availability and benefited the resident raven population.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Raven–Wolf Relationship in Yellowstone</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The most scientifically important aspect of Yellowstone's raven ecology is the documented relationship with grey wolves. Stahler, Heinrich, and Crabtree (2002) studied this relationship systematically in Yellowstone and found:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ravens followed wolves during hunts, waiting at a distance during the chase and kill.</li>
      <li>Ravens arrived at fresh wolf kills typically within 10–30 minutes of wolves beginning to feed.</li>
      <li>At large ungulate kills (elk, bison), raven numbers could reach 20–30+ birds within hours.</li>
      <li>Ravens obtained a significant proportion of their winter food from wolf kills — estimated to consume 15–30% of kill biomass at some carcasses.</li>
      <li>Ravens showed play behaviour near wolves — aerial acrobatics, object-dropping, and occasionally landing in the vicinity of resting packs, apparently without predator–prey tension.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This relationship benefits ravens directly (reliable access to large carcasses opened by wolves) and may benefit wolves indirectly (ravens act as early-warning systems alerting wolves to competing scavengers at kills).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Yellowstone Ravens in Winter</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Winter is the ecologically critical season for Yellowstone ravens. The cold, snow-covered conditions reduce invertebrate availability, making large carrion the dominant food source. The wolf reintroduction in 1995 significantly changed the winter food landscape: prior to wolves, large ungulate mortality was lower and more randomly distributed. With wolves killing elk and bison regularly through winter, ravens gained a more consistent and predictable carrion supply.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Non-breeding raven flocks arrive in Yellowstone in autumn and winter, boosting the resident population. These wintering birds exploit the same carcass resources as residents and participate in the information-centre dynamics described by Heinrich — following other ravens from communal roost sites to productive kill sites each morning.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Other Yellowstone Raven Food Sources</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> While wolf kills are the most studied food source, Yellowstone ravens exploit multiple resources:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Bear kills and grizzly bear scavenging (ravens follow grizzlies as well as wolves).</li>
      <li>Natural ungulate mortality — animals dying from cold, starvation, and disease.</li>
      <li>Geothermal areas — Yellowstone's hot springs and thermal zones concentrate arthropods in winter; ravens forage at these warm patches.</li>
      <li>Visitor facilities — park campgrounds, roadsides, picnic areas.</li>
      <li>Rodents and other small mammals during the summer season.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

